Remaining alerts
WaterSim remains monolithic after Deferred, so ordinary displacement/caustics have one-frame latency. Removing it requires splitting producer and composite passes.
Only eight water volumes can be simulated concurrently; additional volumes remain canonical but explicitly unsimulated. Overlapping water has no authored priority.
Foliage terrain density/exclusion authoring, terrain production, and interaction-field passes do not exist yet. SceneDB publication is ready, but those are absent renderer features.
Deferred currently sits at 8/8 fragment storage buffers and 16/16 sampled textures. Post-process selects the best 256 volumes, reflection the best 64 resident probes, and Corona validates a 64-emitter ceiling.
Calling Scene::flush() twice before rendering collapses temporal history; the contract remains one flush per rendered frame.
Several render passes still use the older Box::leak attachment-descriptor pattern, causing a small CPU allocation leak per descriptor call. That is executor-lifetime cleanup, not SceneDB ownership.
GPU-bearing generic SceneStore types remain deliberately rejected; concrete partnered types and CPU-only generics are supported. The derive currently requires the canonical pulsar_scenedb dependency name.
A published custom wgpu::Buffer can only be read-only by contract—WGPU cannot capability-attenuate a cloned handle—but the facade cryptographically/type-wise prevents safe access to built-in buffers.
SceneDB replication is documented as experimental and is not used by Helio’s render path.
Intentional compatibility breaks include opaque custom actor IDs, typed generational sprite-atlas handles, Radiant registry accessor methods with fallible zero-rejecting registration, and the additive VG visibility projection field. These avoid recreating the secondary-authority paths we removed.